2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Golfers × rounds per golfer × balls lost or retired per round, then add gifts and practice use.$us$, $us$~26M Americans play golf on a course, averaging ~20 rounds a year, so ~520M rounds. A typical golfer loses or retires ~1.2 balls a round (beginners many more, good players fewer), so ~625M balls. Add ~15% for gifts, logo balls and practice, and sales come to ~720M balls, about 60M dozen. Sanity check: at ~$30 a dozen that is ~$1.8B, a believable size for the US golf-ball market.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"L.E.K.","round":"first round","est_minutes":12,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-06$us$, $us$How much ad revenue does the network earn from the Super Bowl broadcast?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$easy$us$, $us$# How much ad revenue does the network earn from the Super Bowl broadcast?

