2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Start from the number of EVs on the road and a sensible ratio of EVs per public port; cross-check with stations × ports per station.$us$, $us$California has ~1.5M plug-in vehicles on the road. Most charging happens at home, so one public port can serve roughly 25–30 EVs; at ~28 that is ~54K ports. Cross-check: ~11K public charging locations × ~5 ports each ≈ 55K. Answer: ~55K public ports. Sanity check: that is one port per ~700 Californians, noticeably denser than the US average, which fits California having roughly a third of the country's EVs.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"McKinsey","round":"first round","est_minutes":12,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
