3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Annual haircut visits (demand) ÷ visits one shop can handle per year (supply).$us$, $us$Demand: ~165M males × ~8 cuts a year ≈ 1.3B visits; ~170M females × ~4 visits ≈ 0.7B; ~2B visits a year. Supply: a typical shop has ~4 chairs × ~8 clients per chair a day × ~300 days × ~70% utilization ≈ 6,700 visits a year. 2B ÷ 6,700 ≈ 300K establishments. Sanity check: one shop per ~1,100 people, consistent with a small town main street having several.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"Kearney","round":"first round","est_minutes":12,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
