3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Sheets per person per day → rolls per person per year → × population.$us$, $us$A person uses roughly 57 sheets a day: ~20.8K sheets a year. A standard roll has ~200 sheets, so ~104 rolls per person a year. × 335M ≈ 35B rolls. Sanity check: two rolls a week per person, which matches a family of four going through a 12-pack in about a week and a half.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"BCG","round":"first round","est_minutes":8,"points_reward":70,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-39$us$, $us$How many food-delivery app orders are placed in the US each day?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$medium$us$, $us$# How many food-delivery app orders are placed in the US each day?

Restaurant delivery orders through apps such as DoorDash, Uber Eats and Grubhub per day in the US.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 12 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Adults who use delivery apps × orders per user per month ÷ 30.$us$, $us$~260M adults × ~35% who order through delivery apps ≈ 91M users. An average user orders ~3 times a month (heavy users weekly or more, light users once a quarter), so ~273M orders a month, ~9M a day. Sanity check: at a ~$35 basket that is ~$115B a year of food ordered, consistent with delivery becoming a large slice of US restaurant sales.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"Bain","round":"first round","est_minutes":12,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-40$us$, $us$Estimate the annual US market for pickleball paddles.$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$medium$us$, $us$# Estimate the annual US market for pickleball paddles.

Annual US retail sales of pickleball paddles, in dollars.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 12 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Split buyers into new players and existing players replacing or upgrading paddles.$us$, $us$~14M Americans play pickleball. New players (~5M a year) buy ~1.5 paddles each (often a two-pack): ~7.5M. Existing players (~9M) replace or upgrade about every 18 months: ~6M. Total ~13.5M paddles × ~$70 average (from $30 starter sets to $200+ carbon paddles) ≈ $950M. Sanity check: close to $1B for a sport that exploded in a few years is consistent with how visible it has become in retail.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"L.E.K.","round":"first round","est_minutes":12,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-41$us$, $us$How many people visit the four Walt Disney World theme parks on an average day?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$medium$us$, $us$# How many people visit the four Walt Disney World theme parks on an average day?

Combined daily attendance of Magic Kingdom, EPCOT, Hollywood Studios and Animal Kingdom.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 10 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Estimate each park’s average daily attendance from its size and capacity, then add.$us$, $us$Magic Kingdom is the busiest, ~50K on an average day; EPCOT ~33K; Hollywood Studios ~28K; Animal Kingdom ~25K. Total ≈ 136K a day, ~50M park visits a year. Sanity check: with ~4 park days per trip that is ~12M trips a year, reasonable for the world's most-visited resort.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"BCG","round":"first round","est_minutes":10,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-42$us$, $us$How many birthday cakes are sold in the US each year?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$easy$us$, $us$# How many birthday cakes are sold in the US each year?

Store-bought or bakery birthday cakes (not homemade) purchased per year in the US.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 8 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Everyone has a birthday: split kids and adults and estimate the share celebrated with a bought cake.$us$, $us$~73M children: ~70% get a bought cake, ~51M. ~262M adults: ~25% get one, ~65M. Total ≈ 116M cakes a year, about 320K a day. Sanity check: a supermarket bakery selling ~15 birthday cakes a day across ~20K bakery-equipped grocery stores accounts for most of that.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"Deloitte","round":"first round","est_minutes":8,"points_reward":70,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-43$us$, $us$How many new homes are built in Texas each year?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$hard$us$, $us$# How many new homes are built in Texas each year?

New housing units (single-family and apartments) completed in Texas per year.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 15 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Population growth → new households, plus replacement of old homes and second homes or vacancy.$us$, $us$Texas has ~31M people growing ~1.5% a year, ~465K new residents; at ~2.6 people per household that is ~180K new households. Replacement of demolished or obsolete homes (~0.3% of ~11.5M units) adds ~35K, and vacancy and second homes ~10K. Total ≈ 225K new units a year. Sanity check: that is roughly one in seven new US homes for a state with one in eleven Americans, consistent with Texas leading the country in construction.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"McKinsey","round":"first round","est_minutes":15,"points_reward":100,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-44$us$, $us$How many ATMs are there in the United States?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$medium$us$, $us$# How many ATMs are there in the United States?

Bank-owned and independently owned ATMs in the US.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 12 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Segment locations: bank branches, gas stations and convenience stores, and other retail and hospitality.$us$, $us$~76K bank branches × ~2 ATMs ≈ 152K. ~150K gas stations and convenience stores, about half with an ATM, ≈ 75K. Other locations (bars, restaurants, grocery stores, malls, hotels, casinos, airports) ≈ 220K independently owned machines. Total ≈ 450K ATMs. Sanity check: one ATM per ~750 people, and most Americans can find one within a short walk or drive.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"Oliver Wyman","round":"first round","est_minutes":12,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-45$us$, $us$How much ticket revenue does a Major League Baseball team make in a season?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$easy$us$, $us$# How much ticket revenue does a Major League Baseball team make in a season?

Regular-season ticket revenue for an average MLB team.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 8 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Home games × average attendance × average ticket price.$us$, $us$81 home games × ~29K average attendance × ~$40 average ticket (bleachers under $20, premium seats in the hundreds) ≈ $94M. Sanity check: well under a quarter of a typical team's total revenue, which also includes national and local TV, sponsorship and concessions.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"Bain","round":"first round","est_minutes":8,"points_reward":70,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-46$us$, $us$How many gallons of house paint are sold in the US each year?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$hard$us$, $us$# How many gallons of house paint are sold in the US each year?

Architectural paint (interior and exterior, homes and commercial buildings) sold per year in the US.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 15 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Residential interior repainting, residential exterior repainting and new construction, then scale up for commercial buildings.$us$, $us$Interior: ~131M households repaint about two rooms a year at ~1.5 gallons each: ~3 gallons × 131M ≈ 390M. Exterior: ~90M single-family homes repainted every ~8 years with ~15 gallons: ~170M. New homes: ~1.5M a year × ~40 gallons ≈ 60M. Residential ≈ 620M gallons; if commercial and institutional buildings are ~25% of the total, the market is ~620M ÷ 0.75 ≈ 830M gallons. Sanity check: at ~$40 a gallon that is ~$33B, a plausible size for the US architectural-coatings market.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"Kearney","round":"first round","est_minutes":15,"points_reward":100,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-47$us$, $us$How many Americans go to a gym on a typical weekday?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$easy$us$, $us$# How many Americans go to a gym on a typical weekday?

People visiting a gym or fitness club in the US on an average weekday.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 8 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Gym members × share who visit on a given weekday.$us$, $us$~72M Americans hold a gym or fitness-club membership. Many rarely go; the average member visits ~2 times a week, so on a given weekday ~30% show up: ~22M people. Sanity check: a typical gym with ~4,000 members sees ~1,200 visits a day, busy but manageable across early-morning, lunch and evening peaks.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"Accenture Strategy","round":"first round","est_minutes":8,"points_reward":70,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-48$us$, $us$Estimate the annual revenue of the US car-wash industry.$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$medium$us$, $us$# Estimate the annual revenue of the US car-wash industry.

Annual spend at professional car washes (tunnel, in-bay and hand wash) in the US.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 12 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Segment vehicle owners by how often they use a professional wash, then × average price.$us$, $us$~285M vehicles. ~30% are washed professionally about monthly (~85.5M × 12 ≈ 1.03B washes); another ~30% a few times a year (~85.5M × 4 ≈ 340M); the rest wash at home or rarely. ~1.37B washes × ~$15 average ≈ $20B. Sanity check: ~$70 per vehicle a year across the whole fleet, consistent with the spread of express washes and unlimited monthly memberships.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"BCG","round":"first round","est_minutes":12,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-49$us$, $us$How many chickens are eaten in the US each year?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$medium$us$, $us$# How many chickens are eaten in the US each year?

Broiler chickens raised for meat and consumed in the US per year.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 10 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$Pounds of chicken per person per year × population ÷ edible pounds per bird.$us$, $us$Americans eat roughly 100 lb of chicken per person a year: 335M × 100 ≈ 33.5B lb. A broiler yields ~3.7 lb of retail meat, so 33.5B ÷ 3.7 ≈ 9B chickens a year. Sanity check: ~25M chickens a day, which sounds enormous until you consider wings, nuggets and grocery chicken breasts together.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"Deloitte","round":"first round","est_minutes":10,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US'),
  ($us$US-G-50$us$, $us$How many college applications are submitted in the US each year?$us$, 'guesstimate', $us$medium$us$, $us$# How many college applications are submitted in the US each year?

Undergraduate applications to US colleges per admissions cycle, including transfer and international applicants.

## What a strong answer includes (≈ 12 min)

1. Clarify the scope and units before you start.
2. A clear, MECE structure (top-down or bottom-up) that segments the key driver.
3. A number for every assumption, multiplied through to a single point estimate.
4. A sanity check against something you already know.$us$, $us$High-school graduates who apply × applications per applicant, plus transfer and international applicants.$us$, $us$~3.8M US high-school graduates a year; ~70% apply to college (~2.66M). Selective-college applicants file 6–10 applications while community-college students file one, averaging ~4.5: ~12M. Transfer and international applicants add ~2M. Total ≈ 14M applications. Sanity check: selective colleges each receiving tens of thousands of applications, with hundreds of such schools, is consistent with a total in the low tens of millions.$us$, true, null, 'size', $us${"firm":"McKinsey","round":"first round","est_minutes":12,"points_reward":85,"source":"us-bank"}$us$::jsonb, 'US')
on conflict (code) do update set
  title          = excluded.title,
  type           = excluded.type,
  difficulty     = excluded.difficulty,
  content        = excluded.content,
  hint           = excluded.hint,
  solution       = excluded.solution,
  skill_cluster  = excluded.skill_cluster,
  interview_meta = excluded.interview_meta
where public.cases.market = 'US';

-- Bootstrap: give the US market a daily pair immediately, so international
-- users see a daily case and guesstimate before the first US cron run. Only
-- fires when the US schedule is completely empty (a no-op on every re-run).
insert into public.market_daily_schedule (market, scheduled_date, case_id, guesstimate_id, source)
select 'US', (now() at time zone 'America/New_York')::date,
       (select id from public.cases where code = 'US-C-05' and market = 'US'),
       (select id from public.cases where code = 'US-G-01' and market = 'US'),
       'bank'
where not exists (select 1 from public.market_daily_schedule where market = 'US');

